#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Assemble the Ordinary of the Mass for the browser, under its rights.

Usage: mass-ordinary {check,structure,coverage} [--calendar NAME] ...

Why the Ordinary is not in the calendars
----------------------------------------
`src/sources/calendars/<calendar>/propers.yaml` is a `sections -> masses ->
propers` index and says so of itself: the postconciliar one states that "the
invariant Ordinary is out of scope". Forcing the Ordinary in as a pseudo-mass
would put a celebration with no date into the sanctoral spine, count it into
the propers census totals that two publications carry, and make those totals
lie. (Sibling tool ids are deliberately not written out here: tmt reads a bare
one as an undeclared dependency, and this tool invokes none of them.) The Ordinary is not a formulary. It is the frame the formularies are poured
into, and it gets its own source and its own file.

Two objects, and only one of them holds words
---------------------------------------------
The book's own words live in an artifact under the edition that prints them ---
a TSV, one physical line per printed block, with a hash and a rights record.
The inventory under `src/sources/inventories/<calendar>-ordo-missae-v1.toml`
holds only the *order of sections*, the witnesses, and what is absent and why.
It never restates a line of text that an artifact already holds. That is the
lesson the propers side learned expensively: a hand-typed second copy of an
artifact drifted from it at 45 loci, and every error the site served sat inside
those 45.

Where a rite has no transcribable text at all --- the postconciliar one, whose
English is ICEL's and whose Latin editio typica this project has established no
distribution basis for --- the inventory carries the element skeleton itself,
with each element's received Latin incipit as its identity and an `absent` key
naming the reason. An incipit is a reference, and a reference is a fact. The
prayer is not reproduced.

The third rights state
----------------------
The propers pass publishes a translation whose `rights` is `public-domain` or
`project-created` and withholds everything else. That is two states, and the
ELLC common texts are a third: under copyright, licensed for free use, and
carrying an acknowledgement the licence requires. `licensed-free` is that state,
and it is not a softer `public-domain`: a witness declaring it must carry a
nonempty `acknowledgement`, this tool refuses the file otherwise, and the
acknowledgement is emitted beside every text it covers so it cannot be separated
from what it licenses in transit to the page.

What this tool refuses to do
----------------------------
*It does not decide what may be published.* A witness's `rights` is read from
the inventory and cross-checked against the source library's own record for the
same id; a disagreement is a hard failure rather than a resolved one.

*It does not fill a gap from a neighbour.* An element with no text in the chosen
witness says so. Substituting another translator's words is the confusion a
choice of witness exists to prevent.

*It does not flatten two sections' key spaces into one.* An artifact's element
keys are unique inside that artifact and nowhere else --- three of them recur
across the 1861 Ordinary's six sections, because the book really does say
`Gloria Patri` twice. Emitted keys are therefore `<section>/<element>`, which is
unique by construction and stays readable.

*It does not rank the Eucharistic Prayers.* The postconciliar Missal prints
four and the priest chooses; the default is the first the Missal itself prints,
which is the only order this project can state without preferring one prayer.

Slots: where a proper of the day sits in the frame
--------------------------------------------------
The Ordinary is the frame and the propers are poured into it, and *where* each
proper is poured is liturgical fact --- the same fact for every page that ever
draws this frame. So it is declared in the inventory, beside the elements it
seats propers among, and never worked out by a renderer. A slot names one
element, whether the proper stands `before` or `after` it, the proper names it
receives, and the `locus` in the missal that puts it there.

Three things are checked here rather than trusted. A slot's anchor must be an
element this file really emits, so renaming an element breaks the build instead
of silently unseating a proper. A proper name may be claimed by one slot only,
because two claims are an ambiguity a page would have to resolve by guessing.
And the slots must run forward through the elements in the order they are
declared, which makes the file's own order readable as the order of the rite
and catches a slot moved without its neighbours.

What is NOT declared is as deliberate as what is. A proper the missal does not
place --- an Ember Saturday's later chants, the whole of Good Friday --- gets no
slot, and a page must show it in the missal's own order rather than seat it
somewhere plausible. That is the refusal this layer owes: a seat invented for a
proper resolves cleanly, reads correctly, and is wrong.
"""
